@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .

#
# prima-materia — what the transmission says when nothing came, and whether it
# ever says to stop
#
# One generalization in two clauses, formed on 2026-09-18 after the Papyrus of
# London and Leiden was read and turned out to take up the empty session six
# times over without once treating it as an outcome. The holding already
# carried the same shape in a Latin grimoire and, in the opposite column, three
# stopping rules from two works of the animal magnetism.
#
# It is the counterpart of generalizations/no-failed-run-counts.ttl and not
# another copy of it. That statement is about where the blame for an empty run
# is put; this one is about what the operator is told to do next, and about the
# absence the two columns divide on — whether the transmission anywhere names a
# point at which he is to conclude that nothing will come.
#
# The counter-search against the holding was carried before the node was
# written, as SPEC §16 requires, and it changed the statement once: an earlier
# wording made the first clause about divination in general, and the
# examination of the Ars Notoria's one-month term showed that a transmission
# whose result is knowledge in the operator behaves like neither column. The
# antecedent is therefore the kind of result and not the kind of procedure.
#

pmc:TheStrongerAddress a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "Where the result is an apparition, there is no point at which to stop"@en, "Wo das Ergebnis eine Erscheinung ist, gibt es keinen Punkt zum Aufhören"@de ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where a transmission makes the appearance of a being the result of its operation, it meets a non-appearance with a further and stronger address of the same kind and states no term, count or sign at which the operator is to conclude that nothing will come; where a transmission instead makes its result a change in the condition of a second person which that person reports, it states such a term, gives it as a count of sittings or of days, and names what the term settles."@en ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:PdmCompulsioNonAdventus, pmc:ClaviculaGradusConiurationum, pmc:TroisOuQuatreSeances, pmc:HuitJoursConsecutifs, pmc:CinqJoursAvantDePresumer ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    skos:note "The two witnesses of the first clause and what each does with an empty session. The demotic manual takes it up in six places and every one of them prescribes speech: the boy who does not see the light is made to close his eyes and called to again; obduracy is met by the summons, which is the god's compulsion; obstinacy with the god unseen is met by the compulsion; a god who delays so as not to come in is met by a further cry; a light that has not come forth is met by having the boy address the lamp himself; a light slow to come within is met by a name said seven times, incense, and a great name uttered forwards and backwards; and gods who are inside and give no answer are met by another name said nine times until they inquire truthfully. The Latin grimoire sets its conjurations in a series and makes the trigger of each the failure of the one before, running through the four quarters, the signing of the foreheads, the re-forming of the circle and a final conjuration declared strong enough that spirits bound with chains of iron and fire could not delay to come."@en ;
    skos:note "What the two have in common beyond the escalation is the absence, and the absence is the half that does the work. Neither names a number of addresses after which the operator is to stop, neither names a sign that the being will not come, and neither gives the session a length. A run is therefore not over until it has succeeded, which is why no run can be counted as having failed."@en ;
    skos:note "The three witnesses of the second clause, all from the animal magnetism and two of its works. Five days before one may presume that the operation does not act, and then a second criterion to consult. Three or four sittings of nervous irritation, after which one may presume that the operation is not good for this patient and ought not to persist. Eight consecutive days of twenty minutes at the same hour, after which the operator will be able to form a definitive judgment. All three name a count; all three say what the count settles; and what it settles in every case is something about this operator or this pairing and never about the agent, which is the finding those three nodes already carry."@en ;
    skos:note "The route between the two witnesses of the first clause is not clean and is stated rather than passed over. The demotic manual of the third century and the Latin grimoire of the late Middle Ages stand in one stream: the Greek and demotic handbooks of Egypt, the Byzantine Hygromanteia and the Latin Clavicula are connected by a transmission history that this holding does not claim to have established but does not pretend is absent either, and the tradition instance of the grimoire carries pm:contactRouteDocumented. Two witnesses on one cable is the floor of SPEC §16 and no more than the floor. The three witnesses of the second clause stand in a nineteenth-century European medical polemic with no proposed route to either, so the division the statement asserts runs across a contact route and not along one, and that is what makes the division worth stating at all."@en ;
    skos:note "Counter-search against the compiled holding, carried before this was written. Every node whose wording prescribes what to do when a promised result does not arrive was drawn and read at its place, and the terms searched over the holding were cease, desist, abandon, stop, leave off, give up, in vain, definitive judgment, does not come and never comes. No transmission of the first kind was found that names a term. Three near-misses came back and none of them meets the antecedent."@en ;
    skos:note "The near-misses, because each of them looks at first like a refutation. The Ars Notoria states a term of one month and an outcome due at the end of it, but its result is knowledge in the operator and not the appearance of anything, so it belongs to neither column; the protocol on it stands at examinations/notory-art-one-month.ttl. The Shangqing sun held in the heart names one year, five years and eighteen years, and what is due at the term is a property of the practitioner's own body in daylight, which is again a third kind. And the ascent ritual of the Greek papyrus carries a forfeiture clause — three times in the year, and no longer coming about for whoever disregards the instruction after it has been given — which is a rule about who may have it and not a rule about a session that produced nothing; the same piece prescribes nothing at all for a non-appearance in either direction, and that silence is recorded as its own search rather than counted here."@en ;
    skos:note "What the statement does not claim. It does not say that the transmissions of the second column abide by their terms, and the three witnesses show they largely do not: the five-day rule replaces its own criterion in the following sentence, the three-or-four-sittings rule prescribes trying two or three further operators, and the eight-day rule is to be applied only to subjects selected beforehand for their likelihood of succeeding. What the second clause claims is that the term is stated, which is a property of the wording; the first clause claims that in the other column it is not stated at all, and that is the asymmetry."@en ;
    skos:note "Falsified by a transmission that makes the appearance of a being its result and states a term, a count of addresses or a sign at which the operator is to conclude that nothing will come — a grimoire that sends the company home at dawn, a manual that gives the inquiry three attempts and no more. One such case refutes the first clause as stated. Falsified in the other direction by a transmission whose result is a reported change in a second person and which prescribes, in place of any term, a stronger address repeated without limit. The likeliest places to look for the first are the transmissions this holding carries that already fix an operation to an hour or a day, since a bounded hour is the nearest thing to a bounded session: the demotic manual itself carries nine such datings at pmc:PdmHoraEtLuna and states no consequence for any of them."@en ;
    skos:note "Untested. No pm:Testing points here, which is the state and not an omission. What a protocol would have to do first is fix the population, and the hard part is the second column: this holding carries exactly one transmission in it, so the clause is at present a statement about the animal magnetism wearing the grammar of a class. A second transmission whose result is a reported change in a second person would settle whether there is a division here or only a contrast between two files."@en .
